<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Converted from "Taming_the_Analysis_of_Variance Aug 28.docx" -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-taming-anova" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taming the Analysis of Variance</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Data vary.
      For example, rice yields differ from one field to another.
      ANOVA helps you investigate whether some of this variation is related to
      the characteristics you have measured.
    </p>

    <p>
      A model is a simple description of the pattern of the data.
      The simplest model might use one predicted yield for every field.
      A more detailed model might allow yields to differ by variety, village,
      fertiliser use or field size.
    </p>

    <p>
      ANOVA helps us compare these possibilities.
      It separates the variation explained by the model from the variation
      left unexplained.
      It asks whether adding a feature explains enough to improve the model.
      This helps you find a model that explains the data well without being
      more complicated than necessary.
    </p>

    <p>
      Your research question and graphs suggest which features to investigate.
      ANOVA helps you decide which of those features are worth keeping in the
      model.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-start-with-graphs">
    <title>Start with graphs</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the <alert><em>rice survey</em></alert> data.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <alert><em>File &gt; Open From Library</em></alert>.
              Press the <alert><em>Instat Collection</em></alert> button, then
              <alert><em>Browse</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Choose the <alert><em>Problem Solving</em></alert> examples, then
              the <alert><em>rice survey</em></alert> data, <xref ref="fig-ch5-01"/>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-01">
          <caption>Opening the rice survey data</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image1.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Open From Library dialog with the rice survey data selected
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-02">
          <caption>Rice survey data</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image2.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                The rice survey data in the data view
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        The most important variable in the data, <xref ref="fig-ch5-02"/>, is
        the yield of rice.
        So, examine the Yield column.
        The smallest yield is just under 20 and the largest is just over 60.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <alert><em>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Boxplot</em></alert>.
              Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Tick the <alert><em>Add Points</em></alert> checkbox and change the
              <alert><em>jitter</em></alert> to 0.05, <xref ref="fig-ch5-03"/>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press the down-arrow next to the <alert><em>graph icon</em></alert>
              in the toolbar and choose <alert><em>plotly</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              In the new <alert><em>plotly</em></alert> window, hover over the
              graph to display the summaries shown in <xref ref="fig-ch5-04"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-03">
          <caption>Setting up the Yield boxplot</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image3.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Boxplot dialog with Yield added and Add Points ticked
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-04">
          <caption>Boxplot of all rice yields</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image4.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Boxplot of all rice yields with jittered points
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-ch5-04"/> shows the variability in the yields.
        Each black dot is one rice yield.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The lowest yield is <alert>19.1</alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The highest yield is <alert>62.1</alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The median is <alert>40.55</alert>: half the yields are below
              this and half are above it.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The box contains the middle half of the yields, from
              <alert>30.1 to 49.0</alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              One quarter of the yields are below <alert>30.1</alert>, and one
              quarter are above <alert>49.0</alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Overall, the yields vary considerably, with a typical yield around 40.
        The next question is: <alert>what might explain this variation?</alert>
      </p>

      <p>
        One objective of the survey was to explore whether yields were related
        to management practices and other farm characteristics.
        For example:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Do different rice varieties have different yields?</alert>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Is the amount of fertiliser related to yield?</alert>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Do yields differ between villages?</alert>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Are yields different on larger and smaller fields?</alert>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-yield-by-variety">
      <title>Yield by Variety</title>

      <p>
        Start by looking at the yields for the 3 varieties.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to the <alert><em>boxplot dialog</em></alert>.
              Click on <alert><em>Facet By</em></alert> (so it goes turquoise).
              Add <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Use the <alert><em>down arrow</em></alert> by the
              <alert><em>facet control</em></alert> and change to
              <alert><em>Col + O</em></alert> (O for overall).
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-05">
        <caption>Rice yields by variety</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image5.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Boxplots of rice yields for each variety, with an overall boxplot
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This figure shows the main idea.
        The three varieties have noticeably different yields:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              NEW yields are mostly around 60.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              OLD yields are mostly around 45.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              TRAD yields are mostly around 28.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The combined boxplot, on the right, has a median of about 40.
        This does not describe any of the varieties particularly well.
        Combining all the yields hides an important pattern.
        It is therefore more informative to summarise the yields separately for
        each variety.
      </p>

      <p>
        Starting with graphs helps to reveal patterns in the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-yield-by-village">
      <title>Yield by Village</title>

      <p>
        Try this again but using the Village to divide up the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to the <alert><em>boxplot dialog</em></alert>.
              Click on the <alert><em>Facet control</em></alert> again.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Village</em></alert> and press
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-06">
        <caption>Rice yields by village</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image6.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Boxplots of rice yields for each village
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The pattern between villages in <xref ref="fig-ch5-06"/> is less clear.
        There are no high yields in Kesen or Niko.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking back at the data shows that no farmers in either village grew
        the NEW variety, which generally had the highest yields.
        The apparent village differences may therefore reflect the varieties
        being grown rather than differences between the villages themselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        This graph alone is not enough to conclude that yields should be
        reported by village.
        We first need to see whether Village explains any additional variation
        after accounting for differences between varieties.
      </p>

      <p>
        Starting with graphs helps to identify questions that need further
        investigation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-yield-by-fertiliser">
      <title>Yield by Fertiliser</title>

      <p>
        Next, consider fertiliser.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fert column is a numeric column and with R you can also get
        boxplots by a numeric variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to the <alert><em>boxplot dialog</em></alert>.
              Click again on the facet and remove it.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Click on the <alert><em>Factor (Optional)</em></alert> and include
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> (even though it isn't a factor).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Check the <alert><em>Cut width</em></alert> box and press
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Cut-width groups the numeric fertiliser values into equal intervals so
        that a boxplot can be drawn for each interval.
        It does not change the visual width of the boxes.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-07">
        <caption>Rice yields by fertiliser level</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image7.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Boxplots of rice yields for each fertiliser interval
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Yields generally increase as fertiliser increases, but the highest
        fertiliser level, 3, does not follow this pattern.
        At first, this might suggest that yields level off or even decline when
        too much fertiliser is applied.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, looking back at the data shows that the NEW variety, which
        generally has the highest yields, never received fertiliser level 3.
        The lower yields at level 3 may therefore reflect the varieties grown
        rather than the relationship between Fertiliser and Yield.
      </p>

      <p>
        We will need to consider Variety and Fertiliser together to separate
        their possible effects.
      </p>

      <p>
        It will be useful in our future analysis to consider Fert as a factor
        as well as numeric variable.
        Let's have both!
        You can duplicate a column and convert it using a single dialog.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Right-click in the <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> column and choose
              <alert><em>Duplicate Column</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Click the <alert><em>Change type</em></alert> checkbox, where the
              default is <alert><em>Factor</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Make <alert><em>Fert_fac</em></alert> the new name,
              <xref ref="fig-ch5-08"/>.
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-08">
          <caption>Creating the Fert_fac column</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image8.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Duplicate Column dialog creating Fert_fac as a factor
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-09">
          <caption>Boxplot using Fert_fac</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image9.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Boxplots of rice yields for each level of Fert_fac
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        Because Fert and Fert_fac contain the same values, their boxplots show
        the same pattern.
        We will use Fert_fac later to compare separate fertiliser-level means
        with a straight line.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-simple-anova-tables">
    <title>Simple ANOVA tables</title>

    <p>
      We now use simple ANOVA tables to examine the same examples.
      The graphs showed how Yield varied with Variety, Village and Fertiliser.
      The ANOVA tables show these patterns as numbers.
    </p>

    <p>
      R-Instat's simplified ANOVA option in the Describe menu provides a bridge
      from graphs to statistical modelling.
      Once you are familiar with the main ideas, the Model menu provides more
      advanced modelling tools.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use <alert><em>Describe &gt; Two/Three Variables &gt; Summarise</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      This dialog has several carefully designed features, which we will
      explain as they arise.
    </p>

    <p>
      First: initially the data selector displays all variables.
      You can model a variable of any type, or several variables at once,
      provided they are the same type.
      Once you select your first variable, only other variables of that type
      remain available.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> to the first receiver,
            <xref ref="fig-ch5-10"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      On the left, below the selector the dialog notes that this variable is
      numeric.
      So, the selector now only permits more numeric columns.
      (You may wish to press <alert><em>Reset</em></alert> and repeat this first
      step with different variable types.
      You could try a factor for example.)
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Click on the <alert><em>Second Variable</em></alert> and add
            <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that the data selector now displays all columns again.
      You are choosing the variable by which the first variable(s) will be
      summarised; this can be of any type.
      Once you make your selection, the dialog tells you the type of summary it
      will produce.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Tick the <alert><em>Means/Model</em></alert> checkbox,
            <xref ref="fig-ch5-10"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      On the left, in <xref ref="fig-ch5-10"/>, R-Instat notes you are analysing
      numeric by categorical.
      The summary will therefore be an ANOVA table.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-10">
        <caption>Setting up the Variety ANOVA</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image10.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two/Three Variables Summarise dialog with Yield and Variety
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-11">
        <caption>ANOVA of Yield by Variety</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image11.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and means for Yield by Variety
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-how-to-read-an-anova-table">
    <title>How to read an ANOVA table</title>

    <p>
      The key idea of ANOVA is to split the variation into two parts: the part
      accounted for by the model and the residual part that the model has not
      explained.
    </p>

    <p>
      ANOVA tables all use the same main columns:
      <alert>Df, Sum Sq, Mean Sq and F-value</alert>.
      Once you can read a simple table like this, you can use the same steps to
      read a larger one.
      A larger model will have more rows for the additional terms.
    </p>

    <p>
      The prediction section depends on the type of explanatory variable.
      A factor such as Variety gives a predicted mean for each group.
      A numerical variable such as Fertiliser gives coefficients describing the
      fitted relationship.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-ch5-12"/> provides an overview of how to read the table.
      <xref ref="fig-ch5-13"/> then explains how each value is calculated and
      what it means.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-12">
      <caption>Reading an ANOVA table in six steps</caption>
      <image source="images/ch5/image12.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          <p>
            Annotated ANOVA table showing six steps to read it
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The figure below explains each part in detail.
      The computer performs the calculations but understanding how the values
      are produced will help you interpret the results.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-13">
      <caption>ANOVA table in detail</caption>

      <sbsgroup widths="25% 70%" valign="top">
        <sidebyside>
          <image source="images/ch5/image13.png">
            <description>
              <p>
                The Df column of the ANOVA table
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
          <stack>
            <p>
              <alert>Degrees of Freedom</alert> – How much information is
              available?
            </p>
            <p>
              Variety: 3 varieties (New, Old and Trad) so 3 − 1 = 2.
            </p>
            <p>
              Yields: 36 total yield values so 36 − 1 = 35.
            </p>
            <p>
              Residual: total df 35 − Variety df 2 = 33.
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <image source="images/ch5/image14.png">
            <description>
              <p>
                The Sum Sq column of the ANOVA table
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
          <stack>
            <p>
              <alert>Sum of Squares</alert> – Calculation: (All variation =
              explained by variety + remaining variation)
            </p>
            <p>
              The Total Sum of Squares is 4955.
              This is all the variation in the Yield values.
              Of this, approximately 3530 is associated with Variety and
              approximately 1430 remains as Residual variation.
              About 71% is associated with Variety and 29% remains.
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <image source="images/ch5/image15.png">
            <description>
              <p>
                The Mean Sq column of the ANOVA table
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
          <stack>
            <p>
              <alert>Mean Square</alert> – Calculation: (Sum of Squares / DF)
            </p>
            <p>
              Dividing each Sum of Squares by its degrees of freedom makes the
              values comparable.
              The displayed output is rounded, while R-Instat calculates with
              unrounded values.
              Calculations made from the displayed numbers may therefore differ
              slightly.
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    3530 ÷ 2 ≈ 1760
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    1430 ÷ 33 ≈ 43.2
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <image source="images/ch5/image16.png">
            <description>
              <p>
                The F value column of the ANOVA table
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
          <stack>
            <p>
              <alert>F-value</alert> – Calculation: (Mean Square for Variety /
              Residual Mean Square)
            </p>
            <p>
              1760 ÷ 43.2 ≈ 40.8
            </p>
            <p>
              The F-value compares the Variety Mean Square with the Residual
              variation.
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>If F is around 1,</alert> the differences between varieties
              are no larger than the background variation.
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>If F is much greater than 1,</alert> allowing separate
              variety means produces a substantial improvement.
            </p>
            <p>
              So, 40.8 is strong evidence that allowing separate variety means
              substantially improves the model.
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside>
          <image source="images/ch5/image17.png">
            <description>
              <p>
                The means table from the ANOVA output
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
          <stack>
            <p>
              <alert>Means Table Prediction</alert>
            </p>
            <p>
              Without including Variety, the model gives every observation the
              same predicted yield: the <alert>grand mean of 40.58</alert>.
            </p>
            <p>
              With Variety: each yield receives the mean for its variety:
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    New 59.6
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Old 45.44
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Trad 30
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
              The <alert>rep</alert> row shows how many observations were used
              to calculate each mean.
              The NEW mean is based on only four observations, so it is less
              precisely estimated than the OLD and TRAD means.
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>
    </figure>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-ch5-variety-conclusion">
      <title>Conclusion</title>

      <p>
        This ANOVA provides strong evidence that one overall mean is not
        enough.
        A model with separate mean yields for NEW, OLD and TRAD describes the
        data much better.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>It tells us:</alert> the Variety explains a large part of
              the observed variation.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>It does not tell us:</alert> that Variety caused the
              differences, or that other factors such as fertiliser and village
              are unimportant.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      By the end of this section, you should understand the meaning of the
      values and how they connect to one another.
      If you learn better by doing it yourself, you can use the calculator to
      make each of the terms in the
      <xref ref="sec-ch5-appendix-first-principles" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-two-more-anova-tables">
    <title>Two more ANOVA tables</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Next get the Village and Fertiliser ANOVA tables and use your newfound
        knowledge to read the information and answer the following questions
        for each.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              How much information is used to describe it? (Degrees of Freedom)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              How much of the total variation is associated with it? (Sum of
              Squares)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              How much variation is there per degree of freedom? (Mean Square)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              How large is this variation compared with the background
              variation? (F-value)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Get the other two summaries.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to the <alert><em>Summarise dialog</em></alert>.
              Change the second receiver from <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>
              to <alert><em>Village</em></alert> and use the
              <alert><em>down arrow</em></alert> on the
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert> button to choose
              <alert><em>Ok&amp;Keep</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Then change the second receiver from
              <alert><em>Village</em></alert> to <alert><em>Fert</em></alert>.
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-village">
      <title>Village</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-14">
        <caption>ANOVA of Yield by Village</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image18.png" width="50%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and means for Yield by Village
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The F-value for the Village is 4.2.
        This means that the variation associated with Village is 4.2 times the
        background variation per degree of freedom.
        This suggests that Village is associated with Yield and is worth
        investigating further.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, it does not yet tell us that Village should be included in the
        final model.
        The apparent village differences might be connected to other variables.
        For example:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Do villages grow different varieties?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Do fertiliser amounts differ between villages?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Before deciding whether to include Village, we need to find out whether
        it explains additional variation after allowing for Variety and
        Fertiliser.
      </p>

      <p>
        Village is a factor, so the output gives the grand mean and a separate
        predicted mean for each village.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-fertiliser">
      <title>Fertiliser</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-15">
        <caption>ANOVA of Yield by Fertiliser</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image19.png" width="50%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and coefficients for Yield by Fertiliser
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Fertiliser has 1 degree of freedom because it is being used as a
        numerical variable with a straight-line relationship.
      </p>

      <p>
        The F-value is 51.9, so the variation associated with Fertiliser is
        nearly 52 times the background variation per degree of freedom.
      </p>

      <p>
        This provides strong evidence that adding a straight-line Fertiliser
        term substantially improves the model.
        However, we still need to consider whether the apparent fertiliser
        relationship might partly reflect differences in the varieties grown.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fertiliser is numerical, so the output gives model coefficients:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Intercept = 27.655:</alert> the predicted yield when
              Fertiliser is 0.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Fert coefficient = 8.948:</alert> the predicted increase in
              Yield for each one-unit increase in Fertiliser.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              This model assumes that every one-unit increase in Fertiliser
              produces the same predicted increase in Yield.
              Later, we can examine whether this straight-line assumption
              describes the data adequately.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-simple-anova-is-not-enough">
    <title>Simple ANOVA is not enough</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        These data come from a survey, so they show associations but cannot
        establish causes.
        Variety, Village and Fertiliser may be related; for example, farmers
        growing NEW may also use more fertiliser.
      </p>

      <p>
        A one-variable ANOVA cannot separate these possible explanations.
        We therefore examine the variables together to see what each explains
        after allowing for the others.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-including-multiple-terms">
      <title>Including multiple terms in the ANOVA</title>

      <p>
        Include both Variety and Fert in the model.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Back to Summarise.
              Press <alert><em>Reset</em></alert>.
              Press the <alert><em>3 Variable</em></alert> button.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> to the first receiver.
              Add <alert><em>Variety</em></alert> in the second receiver and
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> in the third.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Check <alert><em>Means/Model</em></alert> and press
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The results are in <xref ref="fig-ch5-16"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-16">
        <caption>ANOVA with Variety followed by Fertiliser</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image20.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table with Variety then Fert
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The table now has two explanatory rows:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Variety accounts for variation in Yield.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Fertiliser accounts for additional variation after Variety.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Residuals contain the variation left after including both variety
              and fertiliser.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The main results are:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Variety accounts for a Sum of Squares of 3530.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Fertiliser adds a further 695.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The Residual Sum of Squares falls to 732.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Together, the two terms account for about 85% of the variation in
              Yield.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Fertiliser has an F-value of 30.4, so it adds substantial
              information even after Variety has been included.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This shows that a model containing both Variety and Fertiliser describes
        the yields better than a model containing Variety alone.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-does-the-order-matter">
      <title>Does the order matter?</title>

      <p>
        Yes!
        The ANOVA table is read from top to bottom.
        When Variety and Fertiliser explain some of the same variation, the
        first term receives credit for this shared variation.
        The second term receives credit only for what it adds after the first.
      </p>

      <p>
        We therefore fit both orders as a check:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Back to Summarise.
              Remove both <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> and
              <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> in the second receiver and
              <alert><em>Variety</em></alert> in the third.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-17">
          <caption>Variety followed by Fertiliser</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image20.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                ANOVA table with Variety then Fert
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-18">
          <caption>Fertiliser followed by Variety</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image21.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                ANOVA table with Fert then Variety
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              After allowing for Variety, Fertiliser explains an additional Sum
              of Squares of 695, with an F-value of 30.4.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After allowing for Fertiliser, Variety explains an additional Sum
              of Squares of 1230, with an F-value of 26.8.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This confirms that each term explains important variation that the
        other does not.
        Both Variety and Fertiliser should therefore be included in the model.
      </p>

      <p>
        We do not need to keep using both orders.
        The fitted model is the same either way, with the same predictions,
        coefficients and residuals.
        Only the way the ANOVA table assigns variation to the terms changes.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-straight-line-or-levels">
    <title>Should Fertiliser be a straight line or separate levels?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        So far, we have asked whether adding an explanatory variable improves
        the model.
        We now ask a different question:
        <alert>What form should that variable take in the model?</alert>
      </p>

      <p>
        The fertiliser information is available in two forms:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Fert</alert> records fertiliser as a numerical variable.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Fert_fac</alert> records the same values as seven separate
              levels.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        These are not two different measurements.
        They are two ways of representing the same information, and they
        produce models with different levels of complexity.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to Summarise.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Click on the <alert><em>Two Variables</em></alert> button at the
              top.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> as the
              <alert><em>first Variable</em></alert> and
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> as the
              <alert><em>Second Variable</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Use the <alert><em>down arrow</em></alert> on the
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert> button to choose
              <alert><em>Ok&amp;Keep</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Change the <alert><em>second variable</em></alert> from
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> to <alert><em>Fert_fac</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-fertiliser-straight-line">
      <title>Fertiliser as a straight line</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-19">
        <caption>Fertiliser represented by a straight line</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image19.png" width="50%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and coefficients for Yield by Fert
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We have already used <alert>Fert</alert> as a numerical variable.
        Its coefficient of 8.948 means that predicted Yield increases by about
        8.95 units for every one-unit increase in Fertiliser.
      </p>

      <p>
        This represents a straight-line relationship: the predicted increase is
        the same across the fertiliser range.
        The line uses only one degree of freedom, explains about 60% of the
        variation in Yield and has an F-value of 51.9.
        This suggests that the straight line captures an important pattern in
        the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-fertiliser-separate-levels">
      <title>Fertiliser as separate levels</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-20">
        <caption>Fertiliser represented as separate levels</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image22.png" width="50%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and means for Yield by Fert_fac
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We can instead use Fert_fac.
        This treats the seven fertiliser levels as separate groups and gives
        each level its own predicted mean.
      </p>

      <p>
        This model is more flexible; it can allow yields to rise, level off,
        fall or follow an irregular pattern.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fert_fac accounts for about 70% of the variation in Yield.
        This is more than the 60% accounted for by the straight line, but that
        is not surprising: a more flexible model will usually explain more of
        the observed data.
      </p>

      <p>
        The important question is not simply which model explains more.
        It is:
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>
          Does the extra flexibility explain enough additional variation to
          justify five more degrees of freedom?
        </alert>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-comparing-directly">
      <title>Comparing the two models directly</title>

      <p>
        Maybe you can guess the next step?
        Use both fertiliser columns in a single ANOVA to see if Fert_fac
        accounts for more variation after Fert.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return to the <alert><em>Two/Three Summarise dialog</em></alert>
              and the <alert><em>Three Variables</em></alert> view.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Put <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> as the
              <alert><em>second variable</em></alert> and
              <alert><em>Fert_fac</em></alert> as the third,
              <xref ref="fig-ch5-21"/>.
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-21">
          <caption>Setting up the direct comparison</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image23.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Summarise dialog with Fert and Fert_fac as explanatory variables
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-22">
          <caption>Straight line compared with separate levels</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image24.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                ANOVA table with Fert then Fert_fac
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        The combined ANOVA shows that these factor levels have:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              a Sum of Squares of 483;
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              five degrees of freedom;
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              a Mean Square of 96.5;
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              an F-value of 1.9.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        After fitting the straight line, the separate levels use five additional
        degrees of freedom and have an F-value of 1.9.
        This is only a modest improvement, so we keep the simpler straight-line
        model.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-ch5-simpler-model">
        <title>Choosing the simpler model</title>

        <p>
          Allowing separate means for each fertiliser level adds little despite
          using five extra degrees of freedom.
          The straight-line model is therefore sufficient for these data and
          easier to interpret.
        </p>

        <p>
          This does not mean the relationship is perfectly straight.
          It means the data provide no strong reason to use a more complicated
          model.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is an important principle in modelling: keep the simpler model
          unless the added complexity produces a meaningful improvement.
        </p>
      </assemblage>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-does-village-add-anything">
    <title>Does Village add anything after Fertiliser and Variety?</title>

    <p>
      We have found that Fertiliser and Variety are useful terms.
      <alert>Should Village also be included?</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      The simplified ANOVA dialog allows only two explanatory variables, so we
      now use the Model menu:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Go <alert><em>Model &gt; Fit Model &gt; Four Variables</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> as the
            <alert><em>Response Variable</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Add <alert><em>Fert</em></alert>, <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>
            and <alert><em>Village</em></alert> as the three explanatory
            variables.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Then click <alert><em>Display Options</em></alert> and uncheck
            <alert><em>Estimates</em></alert> so that only
            <alert><em>ANOVA</em></alert> is selected.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Return to the <alert><em>main dialog</em></alert> and click
            <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-23">
        <caption>Adding Fertiliser, Variety and Village</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image25.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Fit Model dialog with Fert, Variety and Village
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-24">
        <caption>Choosing the ANOVA display</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image26.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Display Options sub-dialog with only ANOVA selected
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      The resulting ANOVA table has two new features.
      It does not include a Total row.
      We included this on the simplified ANOVA for teaching purposes; it is not
      a standard ANOVA row.
      This table also includes a <alert>Pr(&gt;F)</alert> column containing the
      p-values.
    </p>

    <p>
      The F-value and p-value provide related information:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>F-value:</alert> How large is the variation explained by the
            term compared with the residual variation?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Pr(&gt;F):</alert> How unusual would this F-value be if the
            term added no real explanatory information?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      A small p-value, commonly below 0.05, provides evidence that the term
      improves the model.
      A large p-value means the result could easily arise from background
      variation.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-25">
      <caption>ANOVA with Fertiliser, Variety and Village</caption>
      <image source="images/ch5/image27.png" width="75%">
        <description>
          <p>
            ANOVA table with Fert, Variety and Village, including p-values
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Village has an F-value of 0.79.
      An F-value near 1 means that a term explains no more than the background
      variation.
      Village therefore adds little after Fertiliser and Variety have been
      included, so we leave it out of the model.
      The p-value of 0.51 supports the same conclusion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-interaction">
    <title>Does Fertiliser work differently for different varieties? – Interaction</title>

    <p>
      Our current model says:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Varieties have different average yields.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Yield changes with the amount of fertiliser applied.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Fertiliser has the same effect on every variety.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The question we are asking now is:
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Does Fertiliser have the same effect on Yield for every variety?</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      If Fertiliser has the same effect on each variety, the three lines, one
      for each variety, will be parallel.
      If its effect differs between varieties, the lines will have different
      slopes.
      An interaction allows us to test which pattern is better supported by
      the data.
    </p>

    <p>
      To examine this possibility:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Return to the <alert><em>Summarise dialog</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Add <alert><em>Variety</em></alert> and <alert><em>Fert</em></alert>
            (the order doesn't matter here).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Tick the <alert><em>Interaction</em></alert> checkbox,
            <xref ref="fig-ch5-26"/>, and press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-26">
        <caption>Selecting the Fertiliser × Variety interaction</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image28.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Summarise dialog with the Interaction checkbox ticked
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-27">
        <caption>ANOVA with the Fertiliser × Variety interaction</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image29.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table including the Variety by Fert interaction
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      The interaction has an F-value of 0.3.
      This is below 1, so allowing different slopes explains very little
      additional variation.
      We therefore keep the simpler parallel-lines model.
    </p>

    <p>
      This does not prove that the slopes are identical.
      It means the data do not provide a reason to fit different slopes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-selecting-the-final-model">
    <title>Selecting the final model</title>

    <p>
      A statistical model is a rule for predicting an outcome.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, the outcome is Yield and the possible explanatory variables are
      Fertiliser, Variety and Village.
    </p>

    <p>
      ANOVA has helped us make five decisions:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Include <alert>Fertiliser</alert> because Yield is related to the
            amount of fertiliser applied after allowing for Variety.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Include <alert>Variety</alert> because varieties still differ after
            allowing for Fertiliser.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Do not include <alert>Village</alert> because it adds little
            information after Fertiliser and Variety are included.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Do not include separate means for every fertiliser level because
            they do not improve the model enough to justify the additional
            complexity.
            A straight line is adequate for these data.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Do not include the <alert>Fertiliser × Variety interaction</alert>
            because separate fertiliser slopes explain little additional
            variation.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      The selected model is therefore:
    </p>

    <p>
      A <alert>parallel-lines model</alert> for Yield = Fertiliser + Variety.
    </p>

    <p>
      This model gives each variety its own line, but all three lines have the
      same Fertiliser slope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-reading-the-selected-model">
    <title>Reading the selected model</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Once we have selected a model, the <alert>Model</alert> output becomes
        particularly useful.
        Its coefficients describe the model's predictions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's re-create the ANOVA from <xref ref="fig-ch5-16"/> and concentrate
        on the Model part.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Back to Summarise.
              Press <alert><em>Reset</em></alert>.
              Press the <alert><em>3 Variable</em></alert> button.
              Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> to the first receiver.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Variety</em></alert> as the second receiver and
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> as the third.
              Remember, the order of Variety and Fert does not change the fitted
              model, its coefficients or its predictions.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Check <alert><em>Means/Model</em></alert> and press
              <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The results are in <xref ref="fig-ch5-28"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-28">
        <caption>Selected-model ANOVA and coefficients</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image30.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              ANOVA table and coefficients for Yield by Variety and Fert
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This time focus on reading the model part.
        NEW is the reference variety, so the model uses the NEW line as its
        starting point.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Intercept = 47.755:</alert> When Fertiliser is zero, the
              predicted Yield for NEW is 47.755.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>VarietyOLD = −12.069:</alert> The OLD line is 12.069 units
              below the NEW line.
              When Fertiliser is zero, its predicted Yield is
              47.755 − 12.069 = 35.686.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>VarietyTRAD = −21.791:</alert> The TRAD line is 21.791
              units below the NEW line.
              When Fertiliser is zero, its predicted Yield is
              47.755 − 21.791 = 25.964.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Fert = 5.264:</alert> Every one-unit increase in Fertiliser
              adds 5.264 units to the predicted Yield for all three varieties.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        We have now read the model in numbers.
        Next, we graph its predictions to see the pattern they describe.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch5-graphing-the-selected-model">
      <title>Graphing the selected model</title>

      <p>
        Our selected model has one factor, Variety, and one numeric column,
        Fert.
        It allows each variety to have a different predicted yield but uses the
        same Fertiliser slope for all three.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <alert><em>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; General</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> as the
              <alert><em>Y variable</em></alert>, and
              <alert><em>Fert</em></alert> as the <alert><em>X</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add <alert><em>Variety</em></alert> as both the
              <alert><em>Fill</em></alert> and <alert><em>Colour</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press the <alert><em>Add</em></alert> down arrow and choose
              <alert><em>geom_parallel_slopes</em></alert>.
              This adds the lines.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press the <alert><em>Add</em></alert> down arrow again and choose
              <alert><em>geom_point</em></alert>.
              This adds the data points.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-29">
          <caption>Parallel-lines model</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image31.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                General graph dialog set up for the parallel-lines model
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-30">
          <caption>Parallel-lines model with observed yields</caption>
          <image source="images/ch5/image32.png" width="45%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Parallel fitted lines for each variety with observed yields
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-ch5-30"/> shows the fitted model together with the
        observed data.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <alert>points</alert> are the observed yields.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The <alert>lines</alert> are the yields predicted by the model.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The common upward slope shows the relationship between Fertiliser
              and Yield.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The vertical distance between the lines shows the adjusted
              differences between varieties.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The shaded areas show the uncertainty around each fitted line.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The lines are parallel because the model predicts the same 5.3-unit
        increase in Yield for every variety.
        The differences between varieties therefore remain the same at every
        Fertiliser level.
        This does not prove that the true slopes are identical; the data simply
        do not support fitting different slopes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The graph also shows an important limitation: NEW has only four
        observations, all within a narrow range of fertiliser values.
        Its shaded band is therefore wider, showing that its fitted line is
        estimated less precisely.
        More observations across a wider range of fertiliser levels would be
        needed to investigate the NEW fertiliser relationship properly.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-reflections">
    <title>Reflections</title>

    <p>
      Data vary.
      ANOVA helps us investigate that variation by separating it into:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            variation accounted for by the model; and
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            residual variation that the model has not explained.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful model explains an important part of the variation without
      becoming unnecessarily complicated.
      The residual variation will not disappear completely because
      observations also differ for reasons that were not measured or included
      in the model.
    </p>

    <p>
      R-Instat introduces these ideas through the
      <alert><em>Describe &gt; Two/Three Variables &gt; Summarise</em></alert>
      dialog.
      The simplified ANOVA output deliberately concentrates on variation, model
      terms and F-values rather than formal significance testing.
      It also shows that a model can combine factor variables, such as Variety,
      with numeric variables, such as Fertiliser.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter has treated ANOVA as a way of thinking:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Begin with graphs to identify possible patterns.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Use simple ANOVA tables to examine one explanatory variable at a
            time.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Combine variables to see what each explains after allowing for the
            others.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Test whether more complicated features, such as separate slopes,
            improve the model.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Retain the simplest model that describes the important patterns in
            the data.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      The final model included Fertiliser and Variety but not Village or their
      interaction.
      ANOVA is therefore more than a test of significance: it is a structured
      way to ask which parts of the variation can be understood and how simply
      they can be described.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same ideas extend to other types of outcomes and more complicated
      datasets.
      Later chapters introduce generalised linear models and multilevel models.
      The ANOVA ideas developed here provide a foundation for understanding
      them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch5-appendix-first-principles">
    <title>Optional appendix: Recreating ANOVA from first principles</title>

    <p>
      This optional activity recreates the Total, Variety and Residual Sum of
      Squares from the data.
      It is not needed to follow the main chapter, but it can help readers who
      want to see where the values come from.
    </p>

    <p>
      Begin with the one-variable ANOVA for <alert>Variety</alert> and use the
      <alert><em>Calculator</em></alert> to reconstruct its three Sum of
      Squares values.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use <alert><em>Prepare &gt; Calculator</em></alert>, then the
            <alert><em>Function keyboard</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Some keys have a star (*).
            Click on the <alert><em>cssq*</em></alert> key.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      This stands for corrected sum of squares – just what is needed for the
      ANOVA.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Add the <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> column and press
            <alert><em>Try</em></alert>, <xref ref="fig-ch5-31"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      This gives 4955, in <xref ref="fig-ch5-31"/>.
      That is (again) the total sum of squares of the yield data.
      It is in the Total line of each ANOVA table.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Store this value in a new column called
            <alert><em>tot_ssq</em></alert> and press
            <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      We are trying to explain as much of the 4955 as possible using a model.
      This can include the Variety, Fert, and other terms if needed.
    </p>

    <p>
      To connect the Total Sum of Squares with the standard deviation:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Return to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Press <alert><em>Clear</em></alert>.
            Choose the <alert><em>Maths keyboard</em></alert> and press the
            <alert><em>sqrt</em></alert> button, <xref ref="fig-ch5-32"/>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Add the <alert><em>tot_ssq</em></alert> column.
            Then press <alert><em>divide (/)</em></alert> and 35.
            (That's the total d.f., so n − 1.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Try</em></alert> to show 11.9.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Call it <alert><em>s.d.</em></alert> for standard deviation and
            press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-31">
        <caption>Calculating Total Sum of Squares</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image33.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator using cssq on Yield
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-32">
        <caption>Calculating the standard deviation</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image34.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator computing the square root of tot_ssq divided by 35
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      This gives the same standard deviation, 11.9, as the standard summary
      calculation:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Back to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Choose the <alert><em>Summary keyboard</em></alert>.
            Press <alert><em>Clear</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press the <alert><em>sd</em></alert> key, and include the
            <alert><em>Yield</em></alert> column.
            Press <alert><em>Try</em></alert> to give the 11.9 again!
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now to the two other lines in the Variety ANOVA table:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Back to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Choose the <alert><em>Functions keyboard</em></alert>.
            Press <alert><em>Clear</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Use the <alert><em>ave_fac</em></alert> key (third on top row).
            Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert>, then after the comma add
            <alert><em>Variety</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Try</em></alert>.
            Give the name as <alert><em>var_means</em></alert>,
            <xref ref="fig-ch5-33"/>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-33">
        <caption>Calculating the Variety means</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image35.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator using ave_fac on Yield and Variety
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-34">
        <caption>Calculating the Variety Sum of Squares</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image36.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator using cssq on var_means
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      Now find the corrected sum of squares of the new
      <alert>var_means</alert> column.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Return to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Press <alert><em>Clear</em></alert>.
            Press the <alert><em>cssq*</em></alert> button.
            Add the <alert><em>var_means</em></alert> column.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Now press <alert><em>Try</em></alert>, <xref ref="fig-ch5-34"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The result is 3528, namely the Variety ssq term in the ANOVA,
      <xref ref="fig-ch5-34"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now for the residuals:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Back to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Press <alert><em>Clear</em></alert>.
            Add <alert><em>Yield</em></alert>, then minus ( − ), then the
            <alert><em>var_means</em></alert> column.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Try</em></alert>.
            Call the <alert><em>Stored Result</em></alert>
            <alert><em>residuals</em></alert>, <xref ref="fig-ch5-35"/>.
            Then press <alert><em>Ok</em></alert>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-35">
        <caption>Calculating the residuals</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image37.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator computing Yield minus var_means
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch5-36">
        <caption>Calculating the Residual Sum of Squares</caption>
        <image source="images/ch5/image38.png" width="45%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Calculator using cssq on residuals
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      Finally the residual term in the ANOVA.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Back, one last time, to the <alert><em>calculator</em></alert>.
            Press the <alert><em>cssq*</em></alert> key and add the
            <alert><em>residuals</em></alert> column.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Press <alert><em>Try</em></alert>, <xref ref="fig-ch5-36"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The result in <xref ref="fig-ch5-36"/> is the residual ssq term, i.e.
      1427, in the Variety ANOVA, <xref ref="fig-ch5-11"/>.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
